1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,080
Ask most AI video tools

2
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:04,140
to explain something and you

3
00:00:04,140 --> 00:00:04,840
get slop:

4
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:06,280
melting diagrams,

5
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:08,060
misspelled labels,

6
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:10,060
footage nobody can fix.

7
00:00:10,060 --> 00:00:12,860
Elucera takes a different route.

8
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:14,760
It doesn't generate video.

9
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:16,420
It writes code.

10
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:19,480
Generative video like Sora and

11
00:00:19,480 --> 00:00:21,400
Veo costs six to forty

12
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:23,240
dollars a finished minute, and

13
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:24,440
still can't draw a correct

14
00:00:24,440 --> 00:00:25,120
diagram.

15
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:27,540
Stock-footage tools are cheap, but

16
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:29,000
every reel looks the same.

17
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:31,100
A human animator gets it

18
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:32,300
right, for a thousand dollars

19
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:33,500
a minute or more.

20
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:36,100
Here's Elucera.

21
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:37,640
I ask how public-key encryption

22
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:38,420
works.

23
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:40,540
First I get a storyboard:

24
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:42,360
every word the narrator will

25
00:00:42,360 --> 00:00:43,980
say, and what each scene

26
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:44,380
shows.

27
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:46,800
Nothing is recorded until I

28
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:47,100
approve,

29
00:00:47,640 --> 00:00:48,760
but while I read, it's

30
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:50,380
already recording the voice and

31
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:51,200
planning the scenes.

32
00:00:51,991 --> 00:00:54,091
Gemini records the voiceover,

33
00:00:54,971 --> 00:00:56,251
Whisper times every word,

34
00:00:56,831 --> 00:00:58,551
and Muse directs each scene.

35
00:00:59,571 --> 00:01:01,751
It picks an engine: Manim,

36
00:01:01,751 --> 00:01:04,911
Remotion, Three.js or hand-drawn canvas,

37
00:01:05,531 --> 00:01:06,511
and writes the code.

38
00:01:07,531 --> 00:01:08,851
Every scene renders in a

39
00:01:08,851 --> 00:01:09,151
sandbox,

40
00:01:09,811 --> 00:01:10,891
and if the code breaks,

41
00:01:11,491 --> 00:01:12,651
the error goes back to

42
00:01:12,651 --> 00:01:13,931
the model and it fixes

43
00:01:13,931 --> 00:01:14,231
itself.

44
00:01:31,042 --> 00:01:33,242
Because every frame is code,

45
00:01:34,002 --> 00:01:35,882
text is always legible and

46
00:01:35,882 --> 00:01:37,482
diagrams are always right.

47
00:01:38,402 --> 00:01:39,062
Don't like a scene?

48
00:01:39,842 --> 00:01:41,362
Redo just that one with

49
00:01:41,362 --> 00:01:42,942
a note, or switch engines,

50
00:01:42,942 --> 00:01:43,742
and rebake.

51
00:01:44,782 --> 00:01:46,362
Every scene that works joins

52
00:01:46,362 --> 00:01:47,802
a library the next run

53
00:01:47,802 --> 00:01:48,402
learns from.

54
00:01:49,053 --> 00:01:50,813
This reel took about three

55
00:01:50,813 --> 00:01:53,553
minutes and cost about twenty

56
00:01:53,553 --> 00:01:54,573
cents.

57
00:01:54,573 --> 00:01:57,153
Elucera: explainers built from code,

58
00:01:57,953 --> 00:01:58,453
not slop.

